Records the decision that x/compliance's ante decorator enforces at the
shield/unshield boundary, not inside a shielded pool, and that this is an
accepted trade-off rather than an oversight.

The decorator reads addresses out of message structure, so a shielded
transfer — which names no addresses by construction — is unenforceable by
it. Shield and unshield are ordinary EVM txs with visible addresses, so
the perimeter is already covered by the module as built. No code change.

Accepted leak: an address shielded before being frozen can unshield to a
fresh unlisted address afterward. Bounded to funds already shielded at
freeze time.

The shielded pool is not allowlist-gated, preserving D6's semantics (the
chain never requires allowlisting to transact; only blocklisting stops a
tx). Taken now because x/compliance (PR #10) is being built and the
boundary would otherwise have been fixed by accident.

- ENGINEERING.md §11: D14 row
- ENGINEERING.md §10.1: what the ante decorator actually reaches, and
  what it does not (in-EVM transfers, shielded interiors, inbound ICS-20)
- ENGINEERING.md §18: compliance rows — mainnet authority is settled, not
  "pending decision"; enforcement-reach row added so the gaps are tracked
- STATUS.md §2, §4: D14 recorded; open-decision count corrected to D1–D14
- STATUS.md header, §2a: PR #10 is open, and its unaddressed genesis
  finding (InitGenesis bypasses the protected-address rule) is logged as
  must-fix before networks/ cuts a genesis

