+### 10.1 What enforcement actually reaches (D6 as built, D14)
+
+The ante decorator reads addresses out of *message structure*: signers, the fee
+payer and granter, and the recipients a message names in a field the chain can
+read (`MsgSend.ToAddress`, an EVM tx's `to`, multisend outputs, and so on). That
+is the whole mechanism, and it decides the boundary:
+
+| Reached | Not reached |
+|---|---|
+| Any tx signed by a frozen address, EVM or Cosmos | Value moved *inside* EVM execution — an ERC-20 transfer to a frozen address made by a contract |
+| A frozen address as fee payer or fee granter | Anything inside a shielded pool, whose transactions name no addresses by construction (D14) |
+| A native transfer naming a frozen address as recipient | An incoming ICS-20 transfer to a frozen address (IBC middleware, Phase 3) |
+| Shield and unshield, which are ordinary EVM txs with visible addresses (D14) | |
+
+**The consequence to state plainly:** freezing an EOA guarantees it can never
+sign again and can never be the *direct* recipient of a native transfer. It does
+not guarantee no token ever reaches it. Contracts that must refuse a frozen
+counterparty call `isFrozen()` on the precompile themselves; the chain cannot do
+it for them, and a design that assumes otherwise is wrong about what an ante
+decorator can see.
+
+**D14 applies the same reasoning to privacy.** A shielded pool is entered and
+exited in the open, so the perimeter is already enforced and the interior is
+already invisible — gating the boundary is not a compromise reached under
+pressure, it is the only thing an ante decorator was ever able to do. The
+accepted leak: shield before a freeze, unshield to a fresh address after. Bounded
+to funds already shielded at freeze time. Recorded in D14, and it belongs in the
+whitepaper next to the risks above, not only in the code comments.
+
